SQL queries do not execute in the order they are written, which is the most fundamental hidden reality that trips up developers and data analysts. While you write a query starting with , the database management engine actually processes the data starting with and to build the workspace first.   
Understanding the hidden inner workings, quirky logic, and lesser-known behaviors of SQL will dramatically improve your database design and query performance.  
1. The True Execution Order 
The database engine evaluates operations in a hidden sequence that differs significantly from your written syntax. This is why you cannot use a column alias created in your  clause inside a  clause—the  clause is processed long before the  clause even exists. 

= Written Order: SELECT -> FROM -> WHERE -> GROUP BY -> HAVING -> ORDER BY
= Actual Engine Order: FROM -> JOIN -> WHERE -> GROUP BY -> HAVING -> SELECT -> DISTINCT -> ORDER BY -> LIMIT / OFFSET

2. The Tri-Value Logic of NULL 
In standard programming, things are usually true or false. SQL uses three-valued logic: TRUE, FALSE, and UNKNOWN. 

• 'NULL' represents a missing or completely unknown value, not a zero or an empty string. 
• Because NULL is unknown, any direct comparison like WHERE column=NULL or WHERE column!=NULL  will evaluate to  and fail to return rows. 
• You must strictly use IS NULL  or IS NOT NULL. 
• The Trap: If a subquery returns even a single NULL value, using a WHERE id NOT IN (subquery) statement will completely break and return zero results. To bypass this, wrap denominators or parameters using the NULLIF function to cleanly turn zero-values into safe nulls.   

3. Positional Sorting & Aggregation Hacks 
